Throughout the semester, you will collaborate with peers and receive feedback on various educational activities, enabling you to refine your personal development and professional projects. Your portfolio is a view on this learning journey—showcasing how you’ve applied knowledge, insights, and skills to your projects. The portfolio explains how you are making progess in the semester's learning outcomes and outlines your next steps into mastering them.

Initially, your portfolio may contain limited evidence of proficiency in the learning outcomes. However, as you progress, you’ll gather more proof of your skills and add it to your portfolio. Your first version might be modest, reflecting early work, while your final version will present all the evidence you believe demonstrates proficiency. This final version should also explain why you’ve successfully completed the _Complex Software Systems_ semester and why you’re prepared for the next phase—whether a minor or graduation.

This page provides guidelines to help you assemble your portfolio. Two key components make up your portfolio:

1. A project overview with your *Reading Guide*
2. Collections of *validated products*. The [[Portfolio Delivery]] pages outline a minimal suggested set of work to include.

To streamline portfolio development and feedback, we use the Portfolio Tool in Canvas.

**Note**: Your portfolio is not about creating a dump of all of your end products. It is not up to the reader relate products to learning outcomes, that's yours responsibility! Besides that, it should also give insight in major decisions you have made to reach your results. It should give an impression of the journey you made. Something like *decision records* might be a tool to provide your readers with some insight on this.

## Reading guide

In the _reading guide_, you describe which professional products you have created during the semester and explain how each product contributes to specific parts of a learning outcome.

When describing the professional products in your portfolio, briefly mention what you did to achieve the result, what feedback you received from teachers (and how you reflected on it), and why you believe the product demonstrates a specific learning outcome. However, the purpose of the reading guide is not to provide an extensive account of your semester activities. 

**Tip**: Keep it concise, like a management summary. Use the _evidence description_ and _evidence comments_ in Portflow for additional details if needed. Always ask yourself: "Could someone else understand the relevance of this evidence?"

With each portfolio delivery, you can add some overall reflection on your progress within the semester. What went well? What results are you proud of? What did you learn? What feedback did you receive and in what way did this affect your way of working? Note that this may not only be related to technical subjects, but also to professional skills like communication, working together in a group, planning or presenting.

Besides looking back and reflecting on your progress, you can also look forward: what are you going to do in the upcoming sprints? What do you think still needs to be done in order to reach the proficient level for the different learning outcomes?

In the reading guide there is space for the student to explain the project and indicate for each learning outcome how that learning outcome has been demonstrated.

In the Evidence section ‘Project Overview’, there is space to submit a Word-document containing the following content:

- Context Description: The student describes the situation before the project started and why an improvement can be made here. Who encountered what issues, and why can it be better? (1/2 p.)
- Process Description: The student describes the steps taken from the moment the assignment was received. An easy structure is to follow the phases indicated in ‘Deliverables’. (1&1/2 p.)
- Self evaluation: A general reflection on this process can be done per phase if that is more comfortable for the student. The evaluation can also be done per sprint or portfolio iteration. It should include self assessment per learning outcome. (1 p.)

This text also refers to the products belonging to the various phases. In principle, all submitted products should be referenced.

Please submit a Word-document. The reason it is a Word-document is that it resembles what you will submit during graduation. You can immediately look at the layout. It also provides the language teacher with a clear product to review. Make sure to ask for feedback on it.

In the Learning Outcomes, a free text describes what you have submitted to prove that learning outcome. This also references the products, which are made clickable so the assessor can easily find the products.

## Validated products

The portfolio should contain all validated products that you created in order to deliver successful projects. What products (and in what order) you include is up to you, but the product should clearly be of value to projects. The portfolio is not a complete information dump of everything remotely related to projects.

In order to keep the portfolio clear and easy to browse through, it is a good idea to setup a folder structure that either relates to the different steps of the software development process (analysis, design, realisation, etc.) or the different learning outcomes. Furthermore, when including code, please make sure to include code that is relevant and clearly contributes to the learning outcomes and make sure that the code is properly cleaned (e.g. no .git folders, build output or 800 MB of node_modules). 

## Portfolio (Portflow) tips

You can use the @ mark in the description fields to refer directly to added evidence. This inserts a direct link to your evidence from the description.

Evidence can be linked to multiple learning outcomes!

In the tab 'My Evidence' you get an overview of all your evidence and to which learning outcomes they are coupled. On this same page you can easily manage the relation between evidence and learning outcomes.

Use your evidence description to explain shortly why and how your evidence is relevant to the specific learning outcome(s).

Evidence can change during the semester. Don't be afraid to add evidence that feels not 'complete'.

Your evidence should have received feedback from a teacher or expert, before you can make claims about if it is sufficient or not to contribute to your learning otucomes.